    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the labor law, in relation to the definition of employer for\npurposes of the unemployment insurance law",
    "basePrintNo": "S6469",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S6469-2019",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §512, Lab L",
    "lawSection": "Labor Law",
    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S6469\n\nSPONSOR: RAMOS\n \nTITLE OF BILL:  An act to amend the labor law, in relation to the\ndefinition of employer for purposes of the unemployment insurance law\n\n \nPURPOSE OF THE BILL:\n\nThis bill would amend Article 18 of the Labor Law to establish the\n\"employer\" among multiple entities as the entity that pays an individual\nfor services performed when the supervision, direction, and control is\nexercised jointly by the referring entity and by clients of an entity\nthat refers and pays workers.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1 of this bill would add a new subsection (4) to Labor Law § 512\nestablishing the \"employer\" among multiple entities as the entity that\npays an individual for services performed that constitute employment.\nThis bill would allow an exception based upon written contractual agree-\nments.\n\nSection 1 of this bill would also provide that among the multiple enti-\nties, if one entity provides payment, the experience rating of that\npayment entity will transfer to a new payment entity.\n\nSection 2 of this bill would make it effective on the first of January\nnext succeeding the date on which it shall have become law.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nLabor Law § 512 defines \"employer\" and Labor Law § 560 provides condi-\ntions for \"terms of coverage\" but both do not address situations where\nan individual is paid by one entity but performs services for another\nentity. Among multiple entities, each entity may successfully argue it\nis not an employer, and hence not responsible for Unemployment Insurance\n(\"UI\") obligations under Article 18 of the Labor Law.\n\nThis bill would close a loophole in current law that allows employers to\navoid UI contributions in situations involving multiple entities where\nan individual is paid by one entity and provides services to another\nentity (e.g., employment agencies and their client businesses).\n\nUnder current law, one entity may argue an absence of direction, super-\nvision, and control over an individual that, for example, the entity has\nplaced with another entity. Alternatively, another entity, may argue\nthat it is not the entity that paid the individual for the services that\nconstitute employment.\n\nFor example, in Matter of John Lack Associates, LLC, Memorandum and\nOrder 516638 (3rd Dept., 2013), an agency called John Lack Associates,\nLLC provides waiters to restaurants. The agency explains the duties and\nrequirements of a particular job to individuals on a list, the individ-\nual provides the uniform and equipment, the job site provides the place\nwhere services are performed, and the agency pays the individual. Under\ncurrent law, the Appellate Division was unable to find that the agency\nis an employer. The job sites may be multiple in a situation such as\nthis and holding the job sites responsible is equally challenging.\n\n \nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nThis bill was introduced in both houses as Departmental Bill  61 in 2015\n(A.7504/ S.4850); it passed the Assembly on June 2, 2015, and again on\nMay 3, 2016, but failed to pass the Senate.\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nNone.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis bill would take effect on the first of January following the date\non which it was enacted.\n",
